A||||5||My7Fieros||QUICK COMPUTER QUESTION....||||1||
Z||000000||My7Fieros||05-05-2001||09:22 PM||||Does anyone know how to set up a password for Bios (I think thats what its called).<BR>So when you turn on your computer you have to put in a password even before Windows sets up. TIA.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||btoth||05-05-2001||10:01 PM||||You just go into your BIOS setup... when your computer is starting up, you should see a message on the screen that says something like "Hit F1 to enter setup".  On some computers it's DEL or some other key depending on the BIOS.  Then you should find an area in the setup screen called 'security' or something similar that lets you change your password.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Taijiguy||05-07-2001||09:16 PM||||Not all bios's support startup password protection. Laptops almost always do, but PC's seldom. <BR>You can get security software from Tucows, or download.com that will load after the bios, but before windows, and will prevent access. (many boot managers such as BootMagic support password protection)The important thing to remember is if you really want your computer secure, you have to password protect bios, and set it up so that it won't try to access the "a" drive or cdrom for a boot record. Otherwise, a person could bypass the security measures by putting a bootable disk in either drive. Also lock the case, as a really ingenius person can open the case, disconnect the backup battery, and that would reset the bios to factory defaults, including no passowrd protection to the bios. (anyone tell my girlfriend is a network/internet security specialist?)||||reg||1
Z||000003||baldlobo||05-07-2001||10:09 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Taijiguy:<BR><B>Not all bios's support startup password protection. Laptops almost always do, but PC's seldom. <BR>You can get security software from Tucows, or download.com that will load after the bios, but before windows, and will prevent access. (many boot managers such as BootMagic support password protection)The important thing to remember is if you really want your computer secure, you have to password protect bios, and set it up so that it won't try to access the "a" drive or cdrom for a boot record. Otherwise, a person could bypass the security measures by putting a bootable disk in either drive. Also lock the case, as a really ingenius person can open the case, disconnect the backup battery, and that would reset the bios to factory defaults, including no passowrd protection to the bios. (anyone tell my girlfriend is a network/internet security specialist?)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>you don't have to pull the battery just flip a dip switch or move a jumper over.(hardware junky)||||reg||1
Z||000004||mckay_lee||05-08-2001||12:22 AM||||well i dont know what the hell Taijiguy is talking about, seldom seeing bios support startup password protection for PC's. I have yet to see a PC that does not support bios passwords you just have to know where to look in bios trust me its there.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Taijiguy||05-08-2001||07:53 PM||||Obviously you <I>don't</I> know what the hell I'm talking about. Your condescending comment aside, as far as I know, none of the numerous PC motherboards I've had in the last 8 years have had startup password capability, including my current motherboard, carry an AM bios, and the two previous with AWARDS. <BR>There may be PC's with that ability, but my experience has been they don't generally.||||reg||1
